"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[7462],{7462:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"testId":"ent-test-2","slug":"ent","subject":"ENT","testNo":2,"durationMin":40,"questions":[{"n":1,"subject":"ENT","text":"What is the instrument shown below?","options":["Meniett device","Barany noise box","Portable audiometer","Higginson syringe"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Barany noise box\\n\\nThe instrument shown is\xa0Barany’s noise box, which masks\xa0the non-test ear while testing bone conduction.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Meniett device is a small, portable pulse generator that can control vertigo in patients with M\xe9ni\xe8re\'s disease.\\n\\nOption C: A portable audiometer is a device used to screen hearing loss in settings such as small clinics and homes. An image of a portable audiometer is shown below.\\n\\nOption D: Higginson syringe is used to wash the antrum after an antral puncture is done. This device is shown in the image below.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/43fdb5673a675dbe4945.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/6ada20a42244a413431a.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/b612d91182c309be7253.jpg"}]},{"n":2,"subject":"ENT","text":"A 49-year-old female from Nagaland presented with right-sided hearing loss. On examination, a dull right tympanic membrane is noted. Enlarged lymph nodes are seen in the posterior triangle of the neck. Which of the following is the management of this condition?","options":["Gromet Insertion and Steroids","Radiotherapy","Wait and Watch","Radical Neck Dissection"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Radiotherapy\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of a patient presenting with right-sided hearing loss, dull tympanic membrane (middle ear effusion leading to a conductive hearing loss) and enlarged lymph nodes in the posterior triangle of the neck (cervical lymphadenopathy)\xa0is indicative of nasopharyngeal carcinoma. Radiotherapy is the treatment of choice.\\n\\nNasopharyngeal carcinoma is extremely radiosensitive, hence treatment of local and regional disease is invariably radiotherapy.\\n\\n• Stage I and II are treated by radiotherapy alone while\\n• Stage III and IV require concomitant radiation and chemotherapy or radiation followed by chemotherapy.\\n\\nNasopharyngeal carcinoma is a malignant tumor of the nasopharynx. The theories for etiology include genetics, infection with Epstein-Barr virus, air pollution, and smoking.\\n\\nIt has three types -\\n\\n1. Keratinizing carcinoma\\n2. Nonkeratinizing differentiated carcinoma\\n3. Nonkeratinizing undifferentiated carcinoma.\\n\\nThe most common presenting symptom is cervical lymphadenopathy, occurring in 60-90% of cases.\xa0The most common site of origin of nasopharyngeal carcinoma is the fossa of Rosenmuller. From here, it can spread extensively in any direction locally. It also can spread lymphatically to the cervical, retropharyngeal, or parapharyngeal lymph nodes.\\n\\nSymptoms include:\\n\\n• Nasal- Nasal obstruction, denasal speech, nasal discharge, epistaxis.\\n• Otologic- unilateral conductive hearing loss, and serous or suppurative otitis media.\\n• Facial pain and reduced corneal reflex- cranial nerve\xa0V. (most common cranial nerve to be involved)\\n• Squint and diplopia- involvement of cranial nerve VI.\\n• Exophthalmos and blindness- cranial nerve II.\\n• Horner\'s syndrome\\n• Cervical lymphadenopathy is the most common presenting symptom.\\n\\nNasopharyngeal carcinoma can present with Trotter\'s triad, which includes:\\n\\n• Conductive deafness (eustachian tube blockage),\\n• Ipsilateral temporoparietal neuralgia due to involvement of CN V and\\n• Palatal paralysis due to involvement of CN X\\n\\nEnlarged lateral retropharyngeal nodes compress the cranial nerve IX, X, and XI causing jugular foramen syndrome.\xa0High-resolution contrast-enhanced CT (HR-CECT) of nose and nasopharynx is the\xa0investigation of choice.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nOption A: Grommet insertion + steroids would only treat the middle ear effusion symptomatically without addressing the underlying nasopharyngeal malignancy.\\n\\nOption C: \\"Wait and watch\\" would be inappropriate for a suspected malignancy, as delayed treatment could lead to disease progression.\\n\\nOption D: Radical neck dissection alone would be incorrect as it doesn\'t address the primary tumor in the nasopharynx, and NPC is primarily treated with radiotherapy rather than surgery due to its anatomical location and radiosensitivity.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":3,"subject":"ENT","text":"During syringing, water jet is directed along ______","options":["Posteroinferior wall","Posterosuperior wall","Anteroinferior wall","Anterosuperior wall"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Posterosuperior wall\\n\\nSyringing: Pinna is pulled upwards and backwards and a stream of water from the ear syringe is directed along the posterosuperior wall of the meatus.\\n\\nThis is derived from 2 anatomic facts:\\n\\n• The posteroinferior wall of EAC is supplied by the vagus nerve. Direct stimulation of this can lead to sudden cardiac arrest.\\n• On manoeuvring the pinna to bring the EAC into a straight configuration, the posterior wall is straighter and easily accessible than the anterior wall.\\n\\nTherefore, the direction of choice for the jet of water is along the posterosuperior wall.\\n\\nComplications\\n\\n• Vertigo – caloric stimulation of lateral semicircular canal if water is too hot or too cold.\\n• Rupture of TM.\\n• Reactivation of quiescent otitis media (if there is pre-existing perforation).\\n\\nContraindications for syringing\\n\\n• Presence of ear infection, TM perforation\\n• Presence of grommet or H/O ear surgery","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":4,"subject":"ENT","text":"A patient with bilateral abductor paralysis is admitted to the intensive care unit. In order to relieve the respiratory obstruction, a tracheostomy is being performed. Which of the following is not an immediate complication of this procedure?","options":["Pneumothorax","Tracheal stenosis","Apnoea","Aspiration of blood"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Tracheal stenosis\\n\\nTracheal stenosis, due to tracheal ulceration and infection, is a late complication of tracheostomy and does not occur immediately.\\n\\nA tracheostomy is a procedure where an opening is made in the anterior wall of the trachea and it is converted into a stoma on the skin surface. It is primarily indicated when there is respiratory obstruction, secretions, or respiratory insufficiency.\xa0It is also indicated\xa0in an intubated patient after\xa02-3 weeks\xa0to avoid\xa0subglottic stenosis.\\n\\nComplications of tracheostomy:\\n\\nImmediate\\n\\n(at the time of operation)\\n\\nIntermediate\\n\\n(during first few hours or days)\\n\\nLate\\n\\n(after chronic use for weeks or months)\\n\\n1. Primary hemorrhage\\n2. Apnoea due to sudden washout of CO2\\n3. Pneumothorax due to injury to apical pleura\\n4. Injury to recurrent laryngeal nerves\\n5. Aspiration of blood\\n6. Injury to esopgahus\\n\\n1. Reactionary or secondary hemorrhage.\\n2. Displacement of the tube.\\n3. Blocking of the tube.\\n4. Subcutaneous emphysema.\\n5. Tracheitis and tracheobronchitis.\\n6. Atelectasis and lung abscess.\\n7. Local wound infection and granulations.\\n\\n1. Laryngeal stenosis.\\n2. Tracheal stenosis\\n3. Tracheo-oesophageal fistula\\n4. Problems of decannulation.\\n5. Persistent tracheocutaneous fistula.\\n6. Keloid or unsightly scar.\\n7. Aspiration of tracheostomy tube fragments into the tracheobronchial tree\\n\\nMentioned below are some instruments used during the procedure.\\n\\nCuffed tracheostomy tube:\\n\\n• It has an outer tube, an inner tube, and an obturator.\\n• Presence of cuff prevents aspiration of pharyngeal secretions into the trachea and also prevents air-leak.\\n• Cuff should be deflated every 2 h for 5 min to prevent ischaemia and damage to\xa0the trachea and cartilage necrosis.\\n\\nUses:\\n\\n• Tracheostomy in an unconscious patient or in a patient on a respirator.\\n\\nTracheal dilator is used to keep the tracheal edges open after incision in the trachea so that tracheostomy tube can be easily inserted.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/447b7ab20fbd7d1b1239.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/0284b81307f9bf1b7241.jpg"}]},{"n":5,"subject":"ENT","text":"Superior semicircular canal dehiscence syndrome is associated with all except:","options":["Gaze dependent nystagmus","Positive Tulio phenomenon","Positive Fistula test","Sensorineural hearing loss"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Sensorineural hearing loss\\n\\nSensorineural hearing loss\xa0is not seen in\xa0superior semicircular canal dehiscence syndrome (SSCDS)\\n\\nSuperior semicircular canal dehiscence syndrome\\n\\n• Occurs due to the third window effect that gets created because of the thin/ dehiscent wall of the superior semicircular canal.\\n• Endolymph in the labyrinthine system move in relation to sound or pressure, causing an activation of the vestibular system.\\n• Diagnosed in middle-aged individuals.\\n• Noise or pressure-induced dizziness ,chronic imbalance,\xa0avoidance of noisy environments.\\n• Gaze-evoked tinnitus and/or hyperacusis.\\n• Tulio phenomenon(sound or pressure-induced vestibular activation).\\n• Fistula test is positive(Hennebert Sign)\\n• Noncontrast HRCT of the temporal bone can demonstrate the defect\\n• Treated by surgery if there are severe disabling symptoms else conservative medical management.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":6,"subject":"ENT","text":"What type of tympanogram is expected in this scenario?","options":["Ad type","B type","A type","As type"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. B type\\n\\nImage shows fluid in the middle ear, i.e., serous otitis media.\\n\\nIn such condition, compliance, i.e., ease of mobility of tympanic membrane decreases and hence resulting in a B curve.\\n\\nFluid collection is usually secondary to negative middle ear pressure (Eustachian tube obstruction).\\n\\nTympanometry is an objective test of the middle ear and Eustachian tube functions (conductive compartment of hearing). Here, the compliance of the TM, i.e., how much it moves to the pressure differentials applied from the external ear is measured.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/3ce19fddb59245799a13.jpg"]},{"n":7,"subject":"ENT","text":"A 5-year-old child is brought to you with complaints of noisy breathing. On performing direct laryngoscopy, subglottic stenosis is noted. Which grading system will you use to report the extent of the stenosis?","options":["Hyams grading","House Brackmann grading","Myer Cotton\'s grading","Modified Mallampati grading"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Myer Cotton\'s grading\\n\\nMyer Cotton’s grading is used for subglottic stenosis.\\n\\nIt is the abnormal thickening of cricoid/fibrous tissue proliferation below cords. It causes the airway to narrow and presents with dyspnea, biphasic stridor and a normal cry.\\n\\nDiagnosis:\\n\\n• In full-term infants, subglottic diameter <4mm.\\n• In premature infants, subglottic diameter <3mm.\\n\\nThe Myer Cotton’s grading system allows to stratify the stenosis and plan for treatment:\\n\\n• Grade 1: <50% stenosis\\n• Grade 2: 51-70% stenosis\\n• Grade 3: 71-99% stenosis\\n• Grade 4: No lumen detected\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption A - Hyams grading is a predictive tool used in olfactory neuroblastoma.\\n\\nOption B - House Brackmann grading is used as a facial nerve grading system.\\n\\nOption D - Modified mallampati classification is used to assess difficult airway","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":8,"subject":"ENT","text":"A 65-year-old patient presents with right-sided nasal obstruction and occasional blood-stained nasal discharge. His CT and endoscopy findings are shown in the below image. What could be the underlying cause?","options":["Inverted papilloma","Carcinoma maxilla","Esthesioneuroblastoma","Nasopharyngeal angiofibroma"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Inverted papilloma\\n\\nOn the basis of the given images, inverted papilloma could be the most likely underlying cause.\\n\\nInverted papilloma (Ringertz tumor or transitional cell papilloma or\xa0Schneiderian papilloma) is a\xa0benign neoplasm\xa0of the non-olfactory mucosa of the\xa0nose (also called the Schneiderian membrane) and paranasal sinuses.\\n\\nThe lateral wall of\xa0the\xa0nose in the\xa0middle meatus is the most\xa0common site of origin. The hyperplastic papillomatous tissue\xa0grows into the stroma.\\n\\nIt is common in the 4th to 7th decades of life with a male preponderance. It is almost\xa0always unilateral and presents with unilateral nasal obstruction,\xa0nasal discharge,\xa0and epistaxis.\xa0It can invade sinuses or the orbit, the latter of which\xa0causes proptosis, diplopia,\xa0and\xa0lacrimation.\\n\\nOn examination of nose or endoscopy, it presents as a\xa0polypoidal mass resembling simple nasal polyps, as depicted by arrows in the endoscopic image above. A\xa0computed tomography(CT)\xa0scan and/or\xa0magnetic resonance imaging(MRI) show\xa0the location and extent of the lesion. MRI provides additional help\xa0to differentiate associated secretions\xa0in\xa0the\xa0sinus from the actual tumor mass.\xa0A biopsy\xa0is essential\xa0for diagnosis.\\n\\nMedial maxillectomy is the treatment\xa0of choice, done preferably via endoscopy.\xa0Recurrence is common and malignancy has been observed in up to 15% of the cases.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Carcinoma maxilla\xa0arises from the lining of the maxillary sinus and its early features include additional symptoms like facial paraesthesias, pain, or epiphora and depending on the direction of spread of the growth, would cause additional symptoms.\\n\\nOption C: Esthesioneuroblastoma (olfactory neuroblastoma)\xa0arises from the olfactory epithelium in the upper third of the nose. It has bimodal peaks of incidence at 10–20 years and 50–60 years. Unilateral nasal obstruction and epistaxis are the most common symptoms. The tumour also invades the orbit and the surrounding structures, which results in other symptoms like proptosis, headache, epiphora, diplopia, and blurred vision.\\n\\nOption D: Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma\xa0is seen almost exclusively in males in the age group of 10–20 years and is very rarely seen in older people and females.\xa0Profuse, recurrent, and spontaneous epistaxis is the most common presentation.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/1ba80ab47f35aaec81d8.jpg"]},{"n":9,"subject":"ENT","text":"A 35- year old woman presented to the clinic with the symptoms of hearing loss and pulsatile tinnitus. Further examination reveals conductive hearing loss with Rinne test negative. A reddish mass is seen behind the tympanic membrane. What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?","options":["Acute otitis media","Chronic otitis media","Glomus tumor","Serous otitis media"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Glomus tumor\\n\\nThis clinical scenario is suggestive of a glomus tumor.\\n\\nGlomus tumor is the most common benign middle ear neoplasm. It is seen frequently in middle-aged females. The tumor consists of paraganglionic cells from the neural crest. Glomus tumor is an\xa0extremely vascular\xa0benign tumor.\\n\\nThere are two types of glomus tumors: glomus jugulare and glomus tympanicum. Glomus jugulare arises from the dome of the jugular bulb and invades the hypotympanum. Glomus tympanicum arises from the promontory of the middle ear.\\n\\nClinical features include conductive hearing loss and pulsatile tinnitus- earliest symptoms. Otoscopy examination shows a red reflex through an intact tympanic membrane as a\xa0\\"rising sun\\"\xa0appearance since the tumor arises from the floor of the middle ear.\xa0Paralysis of the 9th to 12th cranial nerves can also occur. An audible bruit over the mastoid is heard on auscultation in all the stages of the tumor.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Acute otitis media presents with throbbing earache and a red and bulging tympanic membrane with loss of landmarks on examination. Tuning fork tests reveal a conductive type of hearing loss.\\n\\nOption B: Chronic otitis media presents with mucoid or mucopurulent ear discharge, the conductive type of hearing loss, and central perforation of the tympanic membrane. Middle ear mucosa can be seen through the perforation.\\n\\nOption D: Serous otitis media\xa0presents with\xa0hearing loss, delayed and defective speech,\xa0and mild earaches associated with\xa0upper respiratory tract infections. Otoscopy reveals a\xa0dull,\xa0opaque, and retracted tympanic membrane\xa0with loss of light reflex, a thin leash of vessels along the handle of the malleus, fluid levels, and\xa0air bubbles\xa0behind the tympanic membrane.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":10,"subject":"ENT","text":"A middle-aged man presents with unilateral nasal obstruction. To check for patency of the nasal valve, a Cottle test is performed. In which of the following cases is the test positive?","options":["Nasal polyp","Epistaxis","Deviated nasal septum","Atrophic rhinitis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Deviated nasal septum\\n\\nCottle test is positive in patients with a\xa0deviated nasal septum. It is used to assess the patency of the nasal valve and when positive, it indicates the abnormality in the vestibular component of the nasal valve.\\n\\nIn this test, the cheek is drawn laterally while the patient breathes quietly.\xa0If the nasal airway improves and the obstruction is relieved on the test side, the test is positive and indicates\xa0the surgery will be useful and the patient can be taken up for surgery.\\n\\nThe\xa0nasal valve area\xa0is the\xa0narrowest portion\xa0of the nasal passage. It is bounded medially by the nasal septum, superiorly and laterally by the caudal margin of the upper lateral cartilage, inferiorly by the floor of the piriform aperture and\xa0the nasal valve\xa0is the specific slit-like segment between the caudal margin of the upper lateral cartilage and the septum.\\n\\nConditions with a positive Cottle test:\\n\\n• Deviated nasal septum\\n• Collapsed nasal valve - causes snoring and sleep apnoea\\n• Valve stenosis\\n\\nThe below image depicts Cottle\'s test\\n\\nThe deviated nasal septum (DNS) may be caused by two main factors, trauma or developmental errors in addition to being dependent on racial and hereditary factors. Patients present with nasal obstruction, headache, sinusitis, and epistaxis and may manifest an external deformity as well.\\n\\nAnatomically, DNS can be of various types\\n\\n• Anterior dislocation or the septal cartilage into one of the chambers\\n• C-shaped deformity: the chamber on the concave side of the nasal septum will be wider and show compensatory hypertrophy of turbinates\\n• S-shaped deformity: may cause bilateral nasal obstruction\\n• Spur: a shelf-like projection often found at the junction of bone and cartilage\\n• Thickening: thickening of a septum due to an organised hematoma may also cause obstruction\\n\\nTreatment of DNS depends on the severity of symptoms. If asymptomatic, no treatment is required. When DNS causes a mechanical obstruction, submucosal resection or septoplasty (more conservative) may be done.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nEpistaxis refers to bleeding into the nasal cavity and most commonly occurs in the little\'s area in the anteroinferior part of the nasal septum. Cottle test to relieve nasal obstruction has no role here. Trotter\'s method is used as a first aid to arrest bleeding from the little\'s area.\\n\\nAtrophic rhinitis\xa0refers to chronic inflammation of the nose with atrophy of nasal mucosa. The patients may present with nasal obstruction due to the crusting filling the roomy nasal cavity. Removal of the crust may relieve the obstruction but has the risk of bleeding.\\n\\nThe nasal polyp occurs due to edematous nasal mucosa. It can be an antrochoanal polyp arising from the mucosa of the maxillary antrum or an ethmoidal polyp arising from the middle meatus in the lateral wall of the nose. These polyps appear as globular mass and can be differentiated from turbinate hypertrophy by a probe test. Nasal obstruction resulting from polyps get resolved by conservative or surgical management.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/5c705a5985540384d531.jpg"}]},{"n":11,"subject":"ENT","text":"A 34-year-old man presented to you with complaints of pain over the nose after falling from height. On examination, there is a fracture of the nasal septum. Which of the following instruments would you use during the surgery for this patient?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 2\\n\\nThe instrument used in the above scenario is the Asch forceps (image 2). It is mainly used to reduce a nasal septum fracture.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Luc’s forceps (image 1) is used in Caldwell Luc operation, submucous resection, and polypectomy.\\n\\nOption C: Tilley’s forceps (image 3) is used in nasal packing in the epistaxis.\\n\\nOption D: Citelli’s forceps (image 4) is used for bone punch.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/c85174293977418dbd25.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a7fa46704fc03bfe4284.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a5720603efebd8016528.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/f282b11ef33dd9143c8b.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/89cd6d9a6cb2f965dff0.jpg"}]},{"n":12,"subject":"ENT","text":"Which of the following is not included in the hypopharynx?","options":["Piriform fossa","Vallecula","Posterior pharyngeal wall","Post cricoid region"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Vallecula\\n\\nVallecula is present in the oropharynx and not laryngopharynx.\\n\\nThe hypopharynx or the laryngopharynx extends from the superior border of the epiglottis to the inferior border of the cricoid cartilage. It is separated from the oropharynx by the lateral glossoepiglottic folds.\\n\\nThe anterior wall is made by the parts of the laryngeal inlet, posterior surfaces of the arytenoid and cricoid cartilages.\\n\\nPyriform fossae are present between the aryepiglottic folds medially and thyroid cartilage, thyrohyoid membrane laterally.\\n\\n• They serve as channels that direct the solid and liquid food from the oral cavity into the esophagus.\\n• Branches of internal laryngeal nerve lie beneath the mucous membrane of the piriform fossa. Hence this site can be used for infiltrating the local anaesthetics.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e3921e093afcdbce3425.jpg"}]},{"n":13,"subject":"ENT","text":"Where is the electrode placed in cochlear implant procedures?","options":["Round window","Scala tympani","Scala vestibuli","Oval window"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Scala tympani\\n\\nThe electrode of a cochlear implant is placed in the scala tympani.\\n\\nA cochlear implant\xa0is a medical device that uses electricity to stimulate the spiral ganglion cells of the auditory nerve to restore sensorineural hearing loss. It converts sound to an electrical signal (transduction) and delivers this to the hearing nerve, which bypasses the damaged hearing apparatus. It cannot be used in patients with cochlear aplasia or without the VIIIth\xa0cranial\xa0nerve.\\n\\nCochlear implant:\\n\\n• An electronic device for\xa0severe to profound SNHL\xa0and\xa0who cannot benefit from a hearing aid.\\n• Principle- surgery to place the electrode array within the\xa0scala tympani\xa0of the cochlea.\\n• Indication-\xa0bilateral severe to profound deafness\\n• Prerequisites-\xa0intact 8th\xa0nerve\xa0and higher auditory pathway\\n• Surgical approach- facial recess (posterior tympanotomy) approach\\n• The electrode of a cochlear implant is placed in the\xa0scala tympani.\xa0(Note: In the auditory brainstem implant, the\xa0site of the implant is the lateral recess of the 4th\xa0ventricle.)\\n\\nThe receiver-stimulator\xa0implanted during surgery (under the skin) and the\xa0electrode array\xa0(implanted in the scala tympani of the cochlea) are part of the\xa0internal\xa0component of the cochlear implant. Microphone, speech processor, and transmitter are part of the external component of the cochlear implant, which remains outside the body.\\n\\nCochlear implants replace the non-functional transducer system of hair cells of the cochlea and convert the sound signal to electrical impulses, which directly stimulate the fibers of cranial nerve VIII.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/f8e691dd4ce17a2cfa12.jpg"}]},{"n":14,"subject":"ENT","text":"A patient presents with bilateral nasal obstruction. On anterior rhinoscopy, multiple smooth pale glistening masses are found bilaterally suggestive of ethmoidal polyps. The surgeon plans functional endoscopic sinus surgery to remove them. Which of the following structures shown in the image given below is removed during infundibulotomy?","options":["1","2","4","3"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1\\n\\nThe image above shows the computed tomography (CT) scan of the ostiomeatal complex in the coronal plane. The structure marked as 1 is the uncinate process which is removed in infundibulotomy.\\n\\nInfundibulotomy,\xa0also known as uncinectomy, is the first surgical step in functional endoscopic sinus surgery (FESS). FESS is the treatment of choice for ethmoidal polyps, which this patient has.\\n\\nThe structure marked as 2 is the ethmoidal bulla, and is removed in bullectomy.\\n\\nThe structure marked as 3 is the middle turbinate\\n\\nThe structure marked as 4\xa0is the medial wall of the maxillary sinus.\\n\\nImportant steps in FESS:\\n\\n1. Uncinectomy or Infundibulotomy\\n2. Identification and enlargement of the maxillary ostium\\n3. Bullectomy\\n4. Penetration of basal lamella and posterior ethmoidal cells\\n5. Clearance of frontal recess and frontal sinusotomy\\n6. Sphenoidotomy\\n7. Nasal packing\\n\\nIndications of FESS:\\n\\n• Chronic bacterial sinusitis refractory to medical\xa0treatment.\\n• Recurrent acute bacterial sinusitis.\\n• Polypoid rhinosinusitis.\\n• Fungal sinusitis with fungal ball.\\n• Antrochoanal polyp.\\n• Mucocele of frontoethmoid or sphenoid sinus.\\n• Endoscopic septoplasty.\\n• Epistaxis control by endoscopic cautery.\\n• Foreign body removal from the nose or sinus.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/d81a00e0c08ea19be6f9.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/5cd9b465c5b1a3554545.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/d0c152b135e197df9b5f.jpg"}]},{"n":15,"subject":"ENT","text":"A 28-year-old pregnant woman complains of progressive hearing loss, which aggravated during pregnancy. Pure tone audiometry reveals a Carhart\'s notch and she is diagnosed with otosclerosis. Identify the procedure done for this patient\'s condition.","options":["Stapedotomy","Stapes mobilisation","Lempert’s fenestration operation","Stapedectomy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Stapedotomy\\n\\nThe above image shows a stapedotomy procedure performed in the middle ear.\\n\\nIt can be identified as a stapedotomy procedure by the following features in the image given:\\n\\n• The footplate of the stapes is not removed\\n• The prosthesis is placed by creating a hole in the footplate of the stapes\\n\\nStapedotomy\xa0is the\xa0excision of the superstructure of stapes\xa0and drilling a hole in the footplate & placing a piston in the hole.\xa0It is done\xa0to bypass the immobile stapes\xa0using a piston which will take over the function of conducting sound.\\n\\nContraindications\xa0for Stapedotomy:\\n\\n• Only hearing ear\\n• Positive Schwartze sign\\n• Otitis externa\\n• Meniere’s disease: can damage the inner ear while drilling a hole in the footplate\\n• Young children\\n• Professional athletes,\xa0pilots,\xa0construction workers, divers: Sudden change in pressure can displace the piston and cause vertigo.\\n• Those who work in noisy surroundings: Surgery makes them more vulnerable to noise-induced hearing loss.\\n\\nOtosclerosis is a primary disease of the bony labyrinth where foci of irregularly placed spongy bone replace part of the normally dense enchondral layer of the bony otic capsule. It is more commonly seen in\xa0females\xa0between\xa020-30 years\xa0of age and the hearing loss is aggravated during\xa0pregnancy and menopause.\\n\\nClinically, patients present with painless\xa0progressive bilateral conductive\xa0hearing loss and\xa0paracusis willisii (patient\xa0hears better in noisy surroundings\xa0as other people raise their voice to be heard).\\n\\nTuning fork tests and audiometry will reveal the presence of conductive hearing loss. The bone conduction curve may show a dip, which is maximum at\xa02000Hz,\xa0known as\xa0Carhart’s notch.\\n\\nThe image shown below shows Carhart\'s notch.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOptions B and C: Stapes mobilization\xa0and Lempert’s fenestration operation are older procedures seldom performed these days.\\n\\nOption D: Stapedectomy involves complete removal of the anterior and posterior portions of the footplate, leaving the membrane below the footplate intact. Now the oval window with its overlying membrane is covered with a piece of fascia and the prosthesis is placed over this.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/3fb4753089f0b206b272.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/7b297175ab6b29cc9105.jpg"}]},{"n":16,"subject":"ENT","text":"A young adult presents with intermittent non-foul smelling mucoid discharge from the left ear for the past few years. Otologic examination revealed a central perforation of the tympanic membrane. Which of the following statements is false regarding his condition?","options":["Aural polyp if present, should never be avulsed","Mixed aerobic and anaerobic infections are seen","No evidence of bone destruction in CT scans","Often associated with cholesteatoma"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Often associated with cholesteatoma\\n\\nThe clinical scenario of\xa0intermittent mucoid discharge and central perforation of the tympanic membrane points to a diagnosis of tubotympanic type of\xa0CSOM, which\xa0is not associated\xa0with cholesteatoma. Atticoantral type\xa0is associated with cholesteatoma and it leads to dangerous complications due to its bone-eroding properties.\\n\\nEtiology of tubotympanic (benign or safe) type\xa0of CSOM:\\n\\n• As a sequela of acute otitis media\\n• Ascending infection via the eustachian tube\\n• Allergic conditions\\n\\nBone destruction is not present in this type.\\n\\nCulture reveals mixed aerobic and anaerobic infection.\\n\\nAural polyp if present should be removed and not avulsed before starting local treatment. This is due to the fact that the polyp may be arising from the stapes, facial nerve, or horizontal canal and its avulsion can lead to facial paralysis or labyrinthitis.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":17,"subject":"ENT","text":"A 52-year-old woman presents to the ENT surgeon with a history of vertigo whenever she moves sideways on the bed while lying supine. She feels nauseous and as if the room is spinning. The symptoms subside after a few minutes but return when she moves her head. Which of the following tests is most appropriate to diagnose this condition?","options":["Epley’s manoeuvre","Glycerol test","Dix-Hallpike’s manoeuvre","Caloric test"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Dix-Hallpike’s manoeuvre\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV). The diagnostic maneuver used in BPPV is the Dix-Hallpike maneuver, the gold standard for diagnosing BPPV. The therapeutic maneuver is Epley\'s maneuver.\\n\\nBPPV is one of the common causes of recurrent vertigo, and the episodes are brief (<1min) and are always provoked by changes in head position. The attacks are caused by free-floating calcium carbonate crystals usually dislodged from the posterior canal.\\n\\nA patient presenting with vertigo should be evaluated for ruling out the central lesions (like a tumor in the fourth ventricle, multiple sclerosis, etc) before confirming the diagnosis of BPPV. Dix-Hallpike maneuver helps rule out central lesions and also diagnose BPPV.\\n\\nDix-Hallpike maneuver:\\n\\n1. The maneuver starts with the patient in a sitting position.\\n2. Their head is turned 45 degrees towards the ear to be tested.\\n3. The examiner quickly brings the patient to a supine position with their head 30 degrees below the horizontal.\\n4. The 45-degree head rotation is maintained during the procedure.\\n5. Now the examiner observes the patient for nystagmus.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/4fd1afc4562fd2c9d95c.jpg"}]},{"n":18,"subject":"ENT","text":"A middle-aged patient presents with multiple nodules in the nasal mucosa. Biopsy from the nodules revealed the presence of Miculikz cells and Russell bodies. Which of the following is the diagnosis?","options":["Rhinoscleroma","Rhinosporidiosis","Lupus vulgaris","Granulomatosis with polyangitis"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Rhinoscleroma\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario and the biopsy findings showing Mikulicz cells and Russell bodies are characteristic of rhinoscleroma.\\n\\nRhinoscleroma is a chronic granulomatous disease caused by\xa0Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis\xa0(Frisch bacillus).\\n\\nMikulicz cells are large foam cells with a central nucleus and vacuolated cytoplasm containing causative bacilli.\xa0Russell bodies are homogenous eosinophilic inclusion bodies found in the plasma cells due to the accumulation of immunoglobulins\xa0secreted by plasma cells. Histology also shows infiltration of submucosa with plasma cells, lymphocytes and eosinophils.\\n\\nThe following image shows rhinoscleroma with Mikulicz cells and Russell bodies(marked in black arrows)\\n\\nRhinoscleroma starts in the nose and extends to the nasopharynx, oropharynx, subglottic larynx, trachea and bronchi. It has 3 stages:\\n\\n1. Atrophic stage– Crusting and foul-smelling nasal discharge\xa0similar to atrophic rhinitis.\\n2. Granulomatous stage– Painless, non-ulcerative granulomatous nodules with subdermal infiltration giving the feel of a\xa0woody nose.\\n3. Cicatricial stage\xa0– Fibrosis leading to stenosis of vestibule, pharynx and larynx.\\n\\nTreatment is done by the use of antibiotics streptomycin and tetracycline for 4 to 6 weeks. Steroids to reduce fibrosis. Surgical intervention\xa0may be required to restore the airway and correct the nasal deformity. Treatment is stopped only when two consecutive cultures from the biopsy are negative.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Rhinosporidiosis\xa0is a granulomatous condition caused by Rhinosporidium seeberi characterised by multiple polypoidal growths.\xa0The biopsy shows several oval or round-shaped sporangia filled with spores bursting through the chitinous walls.\\n\\nOption C: Lupus vulgaris is a low-grade tuberculosis infection characterised by chronic vestibulitis and perforation of the nasal septum. Biopsy shows tuberculous granuloma and caseous necrosis.\\n\\nOption D: Granulomatosis with polyangitis\xa0is a chronic granulomatous disorder presenting with crusting, granulation and saddle deformity of the nose. Biopsy shows necrosis and ulceration of mucosa, epithelioid granuloma and necrotizing vasculitis of the vessels.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/de713dfc440f7f2f604e.jpg"}]},{"n":19,"subject":"ENT","text":"A male patient presents with a sore throat and difficulty swallowing for 2 days. On oropharyngeal examination, a grey-white membrane is observed over the tonsils. All of the following are differential diagnoses for membrane over the tonsils except","options":["Ludwig’s angina","Diphtheria","Streptococcal tonsillitis","Infectious mononucleosis"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Ludwig’s angina\\n\\nAll the above-mentioned options are the differential diagnosis for membrane over the tonsils except Ludwig’s angina.\xa0Ludwig’s angina is the infection of the submandibular space.\\n\\nThe differential diagnosis for grey membrane over the tonsils includes the following\\n\\n• Streptococcus & other pyogenic organisms\\n• Diphtheria\\n• Infectious mononucleosis\\n• Vincent’s angina\\n• Leukemia\\n• Candida\\n• Agranulocytosis\\n• Malignancy\\n• Aphthous ulcer\\n• Traumatic ulcer\\n\\nThe image given below shows acute tonsillitis presenting with a membrane over the tonsil.\\n\\nThe image given below shows pseudomembrane over tonsil typically seen in diphtheria.\\n\\nThe image given below shows oral candidiasis.\\n\\nLudwig’s angina is the\xa0infection of\xa0the submandibular space. The space lies between the mucous membrane of the floor of the mouth and tongue on one side and the superficial layer of deep cervical fascia extending between the hyoid bone and mandible on the other. It is most commonly caused by dental infections\\n\\nClinically, patients present with difficulty in swallowing (odynophagia) and\xa0trismus. On examination, the\xa0floor of the mouth is swollen\xa0and the tongue is pushed up and back.\xa0The submental and submandibular space becomes swollen, tender, and has a\xa0woody-hard feel.\\n\\nComplications of this condition include the spread of infection to the parapharyngeal space, retropharyngeal space, and the mediastinum, airway obstruction, septicemia, and aspiration pneumonia.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/f181002b3c42e0dfbeec.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/b7ce4fe4f4d397b61f28.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/03235d570e53bb9d9a0b.jpg"}]},{"n":20,"subject":"ENT","text":"Identify the given instrument.","options":["Post nasal Mirror","Laryngeal Mirror","St. Clair Thompson Mirror","Double hook retractor"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Laryngeal Mirror\\n\\nThe image shows a laryngeal mirror\xa0which is identified by its straight shaft without any bends with a mirror at the end.\\n\\nIndirect Laryngoscopy is done by the use of a laryngeal mirror.\\n\\nThe patient is seated straight opposite the examiner, with the head and chest leaning slightly towards him. He is asked to protrude his tongue which is wrapped in gauze for protection from any inadvertent injury. The tongue is held by the examiner between the thumb and middle finger.\\n\\nThe laryngeal mirror (size 4–6) is introduced into the mouth and held firmly against the uvula and soft palate. It should be warmed and tested on the back of the hand before introduction. Light is focused on the laryngeal mirror and the patient is asked to breathe quietly. Movements of both cords are seen and compared by asking the patient to take deep inspiration (abduction of cords), say “Aa” (adduction of cords), and “Eee” (for adduction and tension).\\n\\nStructures seen on indirect laryngoscopy (in order) are:\\n\\n• The base of the tongue (posterior one-third of the tongue)\\n• Vallecula\\n• Median and lateral glossoepiglottic folds\\n• Epiglottis\\n• Pharyngoepiglottic folds\\n• Aryepiglottic folds\\n• Arytenoids\\n• False vocal cords\\n• True vocal cords\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOptions A and C: St. Clair Thompson mirror is the other name for the Postnasal/ posterior rhinoscopy mirror. It has two bends in the shaft and is bayonet shaped.\\n\\nOption D: A double hook retractor is used to retract soft tissues during surgery.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/013fb71774cf1cb30157.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/57b76ecb8e1d11a2561c.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/c456f26bbe412896feb0.jpg"}]},{"n":21,"subject":"ENT","text":"Which of the following is true about laryngeal cancer stage T1b N0 M0?","options":["One vocal cord involved and fixed","Both vocal cords involved and fixed","One vocal cord involved and mobile","Both vocal cords involved and mobile"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Both vocal cords involved and mobile\\n\\nIn stage T1b N0 M0 laryngeal cancer, both the vocal cords are involved and are mobile.\\n\\nTNM classification of glottic carcinoma :\\n\\nStage\\nDescription\\n\\nT1\\nTumour limited to vocal cord(s) with normal mobility\\n\\nT1a\\nTumour limited to one vocal cord\\n\\nT1b\\nTumour involves both vocal cords\\n\\nT2a\\nTumour extends to supraglottis and/or subglottis, with normal vocal cord mobility\\n\\nT2b\\nTumour extends to supraglottis and/or subglottis, with impaired vocal cord mobility\\n\\nT3\\nTumour limited to the larynx with vocal cord fixation and/or invades paraglottic space and/or\xa0 minor thyroid cartilage erosion\\n\\nT4a\\nTumour invades thyroid cartilage and/or invades tissue beyond the larynx\\n\\nT4b\\nTumour invades prevertebral space, encases carotid artery or invades mediastinal structures","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":22,"subject":"ENT","text":"CNS infections can spread to inner ear through","options":["Vestibular aqueduct","Endolymphatic sac","Cochlear aqueduct","Scala media"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cochlear aqueduct\\n\\nScala tympani is connected with the subarachnoid space through the aqueduct of cochlea. Infection spreads from inner ear to CNS or vice versa through this route.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":23,"subject":"ENT","text":"What is this view called in which maxillary sinus is seen best?","options":["Towne\'s view","Stenver\'s view","Law\'s view","Water\'s view"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Water\'s view\\n\\nOccipitomental view with an open mouth is also called the\xa0water’s view.\\n\\nWater\'s view:\xa0The patient’s head is positioned such that the nose and the chin touch the film while the X-rays are projected from backward. The mouth is usually kept open while taking the X-ray.\\n\\nStructures seen in water’s view are:\\n\\n• Maxillary sinuses (seen best)\\n• Frontal sinuses\\n• Sphenoid sinus (if the film is taken with open\xa0mouth)\\n• Zygoma\\n• Zygomatic arch\\n• Nasal bone\\n• Frontal process of maxilla\\n• Superior orbital fissure\\n• Infratemporal fossa","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/f67e68acca76380974ce.png"]},{"n":24,"subject":"ENT","text":"A young male patient undergoes a submucosal resection. One week later, he presents with bilateral nasal obstruction and pressure sensation over the nasal bridge. On examination, smooth, rounded swelling of the septum is seen bilaterally. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Septal abscess","Septal hematoma","Septal perforation","Septal deviation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Septal hematoma\\n\\nThe likely diagnosis is a septal hematoma.\\n\\nSeptal hematoma occurs when there is an accumulation of blood beneath the periosteum or perichondrium of the nasal septum. The most common cause of hematoma is nasal surgery or trauma. However, it is also seen in individuals with bleeding disorders.\\n\\nPatients present with bilateral nasal obstruction, frontal headache, and pressure sensation over the bridge.\\n\\nExamination, as seen in this patient, will show bilateral smooth rounded swellings of the nasal septum. If palpated, the mass will be soft and fluctuant.\\n\\nThe following image shows the bilateral, smooth, rounded swellings of the nasal septum indicative of septal hematoma.\\n\\nTreatment of septal hematomas involves aspiration with a wide bore needle if small, and incision and drainage if large. The incision can be made parallel to the nasal floor. Removing a small piece of nasal mucosa will aid in drainage.\xa0After drainage, the nose has to be packed bilaterally to avoid reaccumulation.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A -\xa0Septal abscess occurs usually when there is a secondary infection of a septal hematoma. It is characterized by fever, chills, and frontal headaches.\\n\\nOption C -\xa0Septal perforation can occur after nasal surgery. Features depend on the size of the perforation. Smaller perforations can present with whistling while the larger perforations will present with epistaxis and crust formation. An image of septal perforation is given below.\\n\\nOption D - Septal deviation is characterized by chronic unilateral or bilateral nasal obstruction. There is usually an external deformity and associated sinusitis, epistaxis, and anosmia.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/c3f15df0008934d08131.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/070b4dd1a798704b1722.jpg"}]},{"n":25,"subject":"ENT","text":"A patient with impacted wax develops a severe cough while performing the following procedure on him. This is due to stimulation of which of the following nerve?","options":["Facial nerve","Greater auricular nerve","Auricular branch of vagus nerve","Auriculotemporal nerve"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Auricular branch of vagus nerve\\n\\nThe procedure shown in the image is syringing, usually done for the removal of impacted wax. Reflex cough due to stimulation of the auricular branch of vagus can occur during this procedure.\\n\\nWax is made up of sebaceous and ceruminous gland secretions, hair, desquamated epithelial debris, keratin, and dirt. Factors contributing to the accumulation and impaction of excess wax may be a narrow, tortuous external canal, stiff ear, or obstructive lesions in the canal.\\n\\nPatients with impacted wax may present with impaired hearing, tinnitus or giddiness. Long-standing impacted wax may ulcerate meatal skin and result in the formation of a wax granuloma.\\n\\nWax removal is achieved through syringing or other instrumental manipulation. Hard impacted mass may require prior softening with wax solvents in some cases.\\n\\nThe patient is seated, with the ear to be syringed facing the examiner. The patient wears a kidney tray over his or her shoulder and to collect the return fluid, the patient\'s head is tilted over the tray. A stream of water from the syringe is directed along the posterosuperior wall of the meatus as the pinna is pulled upwards and backward.\xa0The ear canal and tympanic membrane must be inspected and dried with a pledget of cotton at the end of the procedure.\\n\\nComplications of syringing:\\n\\n• Vertigo– caloric stimulation of lateral semicircular canal if water is too hot or too cold\\n• Rupture of the tympanic membrane\\n• Reactivation of quiescent otitis media, if there is pre-existing perforation\\n\\nContraindications for syringing:\\n\\n• Presence of ear infection, tympanic membrane perforation\\n• Presence of grommet or history of ear surgery.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/669d76090ed8747bd385.jpg"]},{"n":26,"subject":"ENT","text":"A second-year resident performs the tuning fork tests on a patient with hearing loss. The Rinne test is positive in both ears and Weber is lateralized towards the right. What is the interpretation?","options":["Left-sided conductive deafness","Right-sided sensorineural deafness","Right-sided conductive deafness","Left-sided sensorineural deafness"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Left-sided sensorineural deafness\\n\\nThe patient has\xa0left-sided sensorineural deafness.\\n\\nRinne\'s test is positive on both ears implying that there is no conductive hearing loss. But there could still be sensorineural hearing loss in either or both ears.\\n\\nWeber\'s test lateralized towards the right\xa0indicates either right-sided conductive hearing loss\xa0or left-sided sensorineural hearing loss.\xa0Since there is no conductive deafness which was inferred from Rinnes\'s test, the interpretation here is left-sided sensorineural hearing loss.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/62a9c8cf67526370096d.jpg"}]},{"n":27,"subject":"ENT","text":"A 69-year-old diabetic patient has come to you with excruciating right ear pain and right-sided facial palsy. Otoscopic examination of the ear shows granulations on the floor of the external auditory canal. Choose the correct statements regarding this condition.\\n\\n1. It is a malignant condition.\\n2. It can occur in immunocompromised patients.\\n3. Technetium 99 scan is used to monitor the disease.\\n4. Gallium-67\xa0scan is useful in the\xa0follow-up of the patient.\\n5. Prolonged antibiotic therapy is given.","options":["1, 3, and 5","2, 3 and 5","1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","2, 4 and 5"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 2, 4 and 5\\n\\nThe given clinical picture of an elderly diabetic patient with excruciating ear pain, facial palsy, and granulations on the floor of the external auditory canal points toward the diagnosis of malignant otitis externa. The correct statements are 2, 4 and 5.\\n\\nMalignant otitis externa is an infective condition\xa0(not malignant).\xa0Technetium 99 scan is not used to monitor the disease.\\n\\nIt is an inflammatory\xa0condition caused by\xa0Pseudomonas aeruginosa\xa0in\xa0elderly diabetics\xa0and immunocompromised patients. It is characterised by excruciating ear pain, profuse discharge and\xa0granulations on the floor of the external ear canal.\\n\\nNecrosis of bone aids in the spread of infection.\xa0Infection may spread to the skull base and jugular foramen causing multiple cranial nerve palsies.\xa0Facial paralysis is the most common nerve palsy seen.\\n\\nGallium-67\xa0scan is useful in the\xa0diagnosis and follow-up of the patient. It can be repeated every 3 weeks to monitor the disease and response to treatment. Technetium 99 bone scan reveals bone infection, but the test remains positive for a year or so and cannot be used to monitor the disease.\\n\\nTreatment includes:\\n\\n• Control of diabetes.\\n• Toilet of the ear canal. Remove discharge, debris and granulations or at dead tissue or bone.\\n• Antibiotic treatment against P. aeruginosa for 6-8 weeks or more.\xa0If the patient is not responsive, treatment should be based on culture and sensitivity of ear discharge.\\n\\nAntibiotics which are usually effective against pseudomonas aeroginosa are:\\n\\n• Gentamicin combined with ticarcillin (IV)\\n• Third-generation cephalosporins (IV) e.g. ceftriaxone, ceftazidime along with an aminoglycoside\\n• Fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, ofloxacin and levofloxacin) combined with rifampin (Oral)","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":28,"subject":"ENT","text":"Treatment of choice for acoustic neuroma is______","options":["Surgery","Conservative","Radiotherapy","Chemotherapy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Surgery\\n\\n• Surgical removal of the tumor is the treatment of choice.\\n• The approach depends upon site and size of tumor:\\n\\n• Middle Cranial fossa approach\\n• Translabyrinthine approach\\n• Retrosigmoid/Suboccipital approach\\n• Combined","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":29,"subject":"ENT","text":"Match the following signs and the diseases in which they occur.\\n\\nSigns\\nDiseases\\n\\n1. Hennebert\'s sign\\n2. Schwartz sign\\n3. Hitzelberger sign\\n4. Bryce\'s sign\\n\\n1. Laryngocele\\n2. Congenital syphilis\\n3. Otosclerosis\\n4. Acoustic neuroma","options":["1-c, 2-b, 3-d, 4-a","1-b, 2-c, 3-a, 4-d","1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a","1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a\\n\\nThe correctly matched pair is 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a.\\n\\n1 - Hennebert\'s sign in congenital syphilis\\n\\nHennebert\'s sign is a positive fistula sign (nystagmus occurring on applying positive and negative pressure in the external auditory canal) in the absence of a fistula seen in congenital syphilis due to the formation of adhesions between stapes footplate and saccule. It is also seen in a few cases of Meniere\'s disease.\\n\\nThis\xa0is not to be confused with Hennebert\'s phenomenon which is disequilibrium following nose blowing or lifting a heavy object. It is seen in the\xa0perilymph fistula.\\n\\n2 -\xa0Schwartz sign in otosclerosis\\n\\nA pink or reddish hue seen on the promontory through the tympanic membrane is called the\xa0Schwartz sign. It is seen in active otosclerosis.\\n\\nThe image below shows the\xa0Schwartz sign.\\n\\n3 -\xa0Hitzelberger sign in acoustic neuroma\\n\\nNumbness in the posterosuperior meatal wall of the external auditory canal (supplied by the VII cranial nerve) is known as the Hitzelberger sign. It is due to the involvement of the VII cranial nerve in acoustic neuroma.\\n\\n4 -\xa0Bryce\'s sign in laryngocele\\n\\nThe gurgling sound produced on pressing the swelling in the laryngocele is known as Bryce\'s sign.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/ebceac784cf67847bc6b.jpg"}]},{"n":30,"subject":"ENT","text":"A 12-year-old boy who was treated for acute otitis media 2 weeks back presents with fever, and pain behind his right ear. On examination, tenderness is present over the mastoid and the pinna is pushed anteriorly suggesting acute mastoiditis. A fluctuant swelling is present between the tip of the mastoid and the angle of the jaw. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Zygomatic abscess","Bezold\'s abscess","Luc abscess","Citelli’s abscess"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Bezold\'s abscess\\n\\nThe above clinical scenario along is most likely suggestive of the diagnosis of Bezold\'s abscess.\\n\\nBezold abscess\xa0is defined as a complication of acute coalescent mastoiditis and is formed when\xa0pus breaks inferiorly, through the thin medial aspect of the tip of the mastoid and collects in the upper part of the neck, most commonly into the sheath of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. The abscess can also take any of the following locations:\\n\\n• Deep to the sternocleidomastoid\\n• Along the posterior belly of the digastric presenting as a swelling in the tip of the mastoid or the angle of the jaw\\n• The upper part of the posterior triangle\\n• Parapharyngeal space\\n• Along with the carotid vessels.\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\n1. Cortical mastoidectomy for coalescent mastoiditis\\n2. Drainage of the abscess in the neck with insertion of a drain through a separate incision\\n3. IV antibiotics\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Zygomatic abscess\xa0forms due to\xa0infection of the zygomatic air cells. It is situated at the posterior root of the zygoma.\xa0 Patients present with swelling in front and above the pinna with associated edema over the upper eyelid. Here the pus collects either superficial or deep to the temporalis muscle.\\n\\nOption C: Luc abscess is defined as a subperiosteal temporal abscess. It develops following the spread of infection from the middle ear along the external auditory meatus, directly, to the temporal region. Swelling is present in the deep part of the bony meatus.\\n\\nOption D: Citelli’s abscess\xa0is formed posterior to the mastoid, occupying a position more towards the occipital bone.\xa0It is also considered an abscess of the\xa0digastric triangle.\xa0In any case, a diagnosis of Bezold abscess is more appropriate for this patient owing to the location of the abscess.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/cec1d6a296de45c21e00.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/3a5b930a400871c7af93.jpg"}]}]}')}}]);